From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 23:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VideoRenderers: place subtitles with a steady first line

Disc subtitles are drawn where they were authored, and a UHD Blu-ray puts
them over the picture: one scope title's English SDH bottom line ends at
y=923 of its 1080 canvas, above the 139-pixel black bar, and its first line
jumps 91 pixels between a one-line subtitle (top at 856) and a two-line one
(top at 765). Text subtitles do the same from the other end: libass anchors a
block at its last line and grows it upward. Kodi's own position setting only
moves text subtitles, and only by their last line.

Add Player > Subtitles > Placement, for image and text subtitles alike:

- Black bars (the default): subtitles in the lower third are moved as one
  block so that a two-line subtitle ends 20 pixels above the bottom of the
  display, with every subtitle's first line where a two-line subtitle's
  would be; those in the upper third go to 20 pixels below the top,
  whatever their height.
- Off: as before.

Subtitles are only moved, never resized, and by whole screen pixels, so an
image subtitle keeps its pixel-aligned draw. The two-line height is learned
from the first two-line subtitle of a file and kept across seeks; until then
it is guessed from a single line. A new canvas, frame, screen size or zoom
starts it over. Image subtitles are counted in lines by their
height on the canvas, text ones against the subtitle font's line height and
line spacing. Text is moved while libass's output is turned into a texture
and put back afterwards, so libass's own change detection still sees the
frame it made and does not rebuild the texture every frame.
Top and bottom blocks move independently, and lines authored as separate
objects move together.

Anything the rule cannot place is left where it was authored: subtitles in
the middle of the picture, bottom blocks of more than two lines, mixed canvases,
placements other than on the video or the screen (cropped encodes, whose
subtitles keep the canvas's aspect ratio on the screen, are covered), text blocks that would
leave the frame, and stereoscopic output. Positioned ASS signs in the lower
or upper third are moved with the block; the rendered text carries no mark
telling them apart.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
